<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakijin Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nakijin Gusuku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakijin Castle is a gusuku, or Okinawan fortress, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motobu-peninsula">Motobu Peninsula</a> of Okinawa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It was the seat of the kings of Hokuzan until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a> of Chūzan captured it in 1416, and it later served as the residence of a royal warden for the north. Its long, winding limestone walls survive, and it is part of a World Heritage Site inscribed in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakijin-castle.jpg" alt="Nakijin Castle" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakijin_Okinawa_Japan_Nakikjin-Castle-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
